NodePort:
A NodePort does everything a ClusterIP does - it still gives your pods one steady internal address - and then it does one more thing:
it opens the same port number on every node of the cluster. Any traffic that arrives at a node's IP on that port is forwarded inward to your pods.

Three ports, do not mix them up
A NodePort Service actually names three ports, and it helps to keep them straight:

targetPort - the port your container listens on (80 for nginx). ---------------- container
port - the port the Service itself answers on inside the cluster. -------------- 
nodePort - the port opened on every node for the outside world.



kubectl create deployment web-lab --image=nginx:alpine
kubectl rollout status deployment/web-lab --timeout=20s
kubectl expose deployment web-lab --name=web-np --type=NodePort --port=80
kubectl get svc web-np


