=======================LOAD Balancer without cloud connection========================================================
For a production app you usually want one clean, stable, public address, with traffic spread across your nodes automatically. That is what a LoadBalancer 
kubectl expose with --type=LoadBalancer - request a cloud load balancer Service (its external IP stays pending on kind

example:
kubectl get deploy web-lab >/dev/null 2>&1 || kubectl create deployment web-lab --image=nginx:alpine

kubectl get svc web-lb >/dev/null 2>&1 || kubectl expose deployment web-lab --name=web-lb --type=LoadBalancer --port=80
kubectl get svc web-lb
=============================Reach some domain outside node=========================================
Sometimes the thing you need to reach is not in the cluster at all - a managed database, a payment API, some service at an address like db.example.com. 
It is pure DNS: a request inside the cluster for the Service name is answered with the external DNS name you set, so the caller is quietly redirected outward.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: ext-db
spec:
  type: ExternalName
  externalName: db.example.com
It does not proxy or load balance any traffic. It only answers a DNS lookup with another name. The actual connection goes straight from your pod to the external host.
It has no port, no nodePort, and no external IP, because it is not opening any door into the cluster. It points from inside to outside.
=========================================SUMMARY ServiceS:====================================
 need access from outside the cluster?
   no  -> ClusterIP          (just testing? add kubectl port-forward)
   yes -> running in a cloud?
            no  -> NodePort
            yes -> LoadBalancer   (HTTP? put an Ingress in front)

 pointing at an address outside the cluster instead? -> ExternalName

